WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.778 --> 00:00:05.560
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:06.340
Hi.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:08.440
My name is Chelsea
Parlett-Pelleriti,

00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:11.380
and I'm on Twitter
@ChelseaParlett in case

00:00:11.380 --> 00:00:15.130
you want to follow any of the
fun stats content that I make.

00:00:15.130 --> 00:00:17.590
And speaking of stats
content, something

00:00:17.590 --> 00:00:20.380
that I've spent a lot of
time making in the past year

00:00:20.380 --> 00:00:23.230
are guided simulation exercises.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:26.260
I teach data science to
mostly undergraduates,

00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.570
and it can be an
intimidating subject to them.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:31.900
I often get asked
for "rules of thumb"

00:00:31.900 --> 00:00:34.960
that they can use to make
good data science decisions.

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:37.480
And while there are
some rules of thumb

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:39.760
that work in a
lot of situations,

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:43.450
I want my students to come
away with a deep understanding

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:46.810
of the concepts so when
they're in a situation where

00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:50.560
that rule of thumb doesn't
apply, they're not just stuck.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:54.220
I decided that guided simulation
exercises were a great way

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:56.950
to create that
deeper understanding.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:00.160
By my definition, guided
simulation exercises

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:03.460
are activities where you're
asked to simulate data,

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:07.690
you apply an algorithm or
a computation to that data.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:09.610
And then you're
given specific guided

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:11.920
questions that can
help you figure out

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.110
what that simulation
can tell you

00:01:14.110 --> 00:01:16.780
about whichever
algorithm or computation

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.520
you applied to the data.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:22.540
Simulation exercises are
helpful for three main reasons.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:26.700
First, I think it encourages
this attitude of exploration

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:27.760
when learning.

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.390
And second, it gives you
tools to test your intuition

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:36.560
or try weird edge cases that you
think of and see what happens.

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:41.260
And finally, I think it
empowers this deep understanding

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:43.120
of the concepts that
you're learning.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:44.890
A lot of people
learn data science

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:46.970
because they think they should.

00:01:46.970 --> 00:01:51.250
But my goal is to help
people really deeply explore

00:01:51.250 --> 00:01:53.260
the concepts that
they're learning.

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:56.290
And sure they could write
their own simulations,

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:59.380
but I think the guided
part is really important.

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:01.630
I think these exercises
are a little more

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:03.820
practical than just
throwing people

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.100
in the deep end on their own.

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:11.290
It makes simulating data in
R feel more approachable,

00:02:11.290 --> 00:02:14.080
and it gives people tools
to try and figure things

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:17.410
out themselves rather than just
relying on what they're told.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:21.370
Relatedly, the guided
nature of these exercises

00:02:21.370 --> 00:02:25.060
gives people the tools to learn
and try things on their own

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.480
and tailor their experience
to things that specifically

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:29.830
make them curious.

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:31.690
For example, maybe
you're wondering

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:33.970
what would happen if I
ran principal component

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:36.040
analysis on a set
of variables that

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:38.410
are completely uncorrelated?

00:02:38.410 --> 00:02:40.030
We'll try it out.

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:42.820
Or maybe you're wondering
why we would ever

00:02:42.820 --> 00:02:46.790
use k-fold cross-validation
rather than the simpler

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:50.200
and more computationally
efficient train test split?

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:54.220
Well run a bunch of simulations
and see what the difference is.

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:56.800
In my opinion, the
best data scientists

00:02:56.800 --> 00:02:59.470
are the ones who have a
little bit of doubt about

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:04.330
whether the established method
is the best way necessarily.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.910
And these guided
simulation exercises

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:10.210
encourage people
to test things out,

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:13.600
to push on the boundaries
of established methods,

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.990
and to figure out how our
favorite algorithms work

00:03:16.990 --> 00:03:19.600
in atypical situations.

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.800
After all, our data will
not always be well behaved.

00:03:23.800 --> 00:03:26.170
Guided simulation
exercises are not

00:03:26.170 --> 00:03:28.090
a replacement for
the math in theory

00:03:28.090 --> 00:03:30.190
that go behind data
science topics,

00:03:30.190 --> 00:03:32.870
but it can help support them.

00:03:32.870 --> 00:03:36.280
And I think the examples,
the specific questions,

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:39.370
and the established code
help make simulation

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:41.710
more accessible,
especially to people

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:44.410
who don't have the
skills yet or maybe don't

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:47.930
have the time to set all
of that up on their own.

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.170
I hope you'll check out
the guided simulations that

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:53.470
are already on the conference
GitHub and all of the ones

00:03:53.470 --> 00:03:55.250
that we make in the future.

00:03:55.250 --> 00:03:57.130
But for now, in
my last minute, I

00:03:57.130 --> 00:03:59.440
would love to give you
the world's quickest

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:02.680
overview of my principal
component analysis guided

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:04.420
simulation exercise.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.940
When I teach principal
component analysis,

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.610
one of the main ideas
that I emphasize

00:04:09.610 --> 00:04:12.430
is that principal component
analysis takes advantage

00:04:12.430 --> 00:04:14.800
of relationships
between variables

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.980
in order to create more
efficient axes that

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.630
better define the
variation in the data.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:22.360
So it shouldn't be
a surprise that when

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.930
we look at principal component
analysis on completely

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:28.780
uncorrelated variables,
the screeplot shows us

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:31.330
that each variable
pretty much contributes

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:33.370
its own independent information.

00:04:33.370 --> 00:04:36.910
On the other hand, when we have
highly correlated variables,

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:39.250
the PCA model as well
as the screeplot

00:04:39.250 --> 00:04:41.510
tells us that a lot
of the variation

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.260
then can be explained
by one single component

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:47.110
because there is a lot of
shared information in the data

00:04:47.110 --> 00:04:48.010
originally.

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:49.955
Thank you so much for
listening to my talk.

00:04:49.955 --> 00:04:52.330
I would love to connect with
all of you over the internet

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:55.300
and hear whether you use any
of these guided simulation

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:59.200
exercises, either for yourself
or for people that you teach.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:02.810
And definitely let me know if
you create any of your own.

00:05:02.810 --> 00:05:04.840
I would love to
hear about it think.

